\b ## Section B: Long Scenario Questions (50 Points)\b0\par
\par
\b Question 33: Proxmox High Availability and Clustering (25 Points)\b0\par
\par
\b Scenario:\b0  You are managing a 3-node Proxmox cluster (pve-01, pve-02, pve-03). You need to ensure that if one node fails, its critical VMs automatically restart on another node.\par
\par
1.1. What is the minimum number of nodes required in a Proxmox cluster to maintain "Quorum" without manual intervention? Explain why. [5 Points]\par
\par
1.2. Fencing:\par
a) What is the purpose of "Fencing" in an HA cluster? [3 Points]\par
b) What is the name of the software-based fencing component used in Proxmox to reboot a failed node? [2 Points]\par
\par
1.3. You want to mark VM 101 as "High Availability".\par
a) What is the Proxmox CLI command (ha-manager) to add VM 101 to the HA manager? [3 Points]\par
b) Which status code in the HA manager indicates the VM is healthy and being monitored? [2 Points]\par
\par
1.4. Shared Storage is a prerequisite for HA.\par
a) Explain why local LVM storage cannot be used for HA VMs. [3 Points]\par
b) Provide one example of a storage backend that Proxmox can use that supports cluster-wide access. [2 Points]\par
\par
1.5. Node Recovery: Node pve-03 lost power. After fixing the issue, you want to see the status of all nodes in the cluster. Provide the command. [5 Points]\par
\par
\b Answer:\b0\par
1.1. 3 nodes. Majority (more than 50%) is needed to prevent split-brain; with 3 nodes, you can lose 1 and still have 2 votes.\par
1.2. a) Fencing isolates or kills a failed node before others touch its disks. b) Watchdog.\par
1.3. a) \f0\fs22 ha-manager add vm:101\f1\fs24 . b) \b started\b0 .\par
1.4. a) Local storage is only on one disk; if the node dies, the data is gone. b) Ceph / NFS.\par
1.5. \f0\fs22 pvecm status\f1\fs24\par
\par
\b Question 34: Orchestration, Containers, and Automation (20 Pts fragment - adjusted to 25)\b0\par
\par
\b Scenario:\b0  You are transitioning a legacy web application into a modern microservices architecture using Docker.\par
\par
1.1. You have a Dockerfile. You need to build an image named web-app:v1 and then run a container from it in the background, mapping host port 80 to container port 80. [5 Points]\par
\par
1.2. Persistence: Your app needs to save logs to the host machine at /var/log/myapp. How do you define a "Bind Mount" in the 'docker run' command to map this host directory to /app/logs inside the container? [6 Points]\par
\par
1.3. Docker Compose: You have a database service and a web service. Explain why you should use 'depends_on' in your docker-compose.yml file. [4 Points]\par
\par
1.4. Networking: How does Docker allow containers on the same host to talk to each other by their "container name"? [5 Points]\par
\par
1.5. Clean-up: Provide the command to remove all stopped containers and unused networks at once. [5 Points]\par
\par
\b Answer:\b0\par
1.1. \f0\fs22 docker build -t web-app:v1 .\f1\fs24  then \f0\fs22 docker run -d -p 80:80 web-app:v1\f1\fs24\par
1.2. \f0\fs22 -v /var/log/myapp:/app/logs\f1\fs24\par
1.3. To ensure the database starts before the web server starts.\par
1.4. Using the built-in Docker DNS server (on a user-defined bridge network).\par
1.5. \f0\fs22 docker system prune\f1\fs24\par
